// THE RAW-POINTER ABUSE DIRECTION. check_recv_conflict's raw exemption tests
// the type of the ROOT, which for `*p = v` with a local `*mut T` IS the
// pointer — the coincidence that made it look right. For `*pp.p = v` the root
// is the STRUCT, so the exemption would stop applying exactly where the path
// becomes non-empty. It now tests the POINTER EXPRESSION's own type.
//
// ⚠ THIS FIXTURE ALONE DOES NOT PROVE THE GUARD, and saying so is the point: a
// raw field with no live borrow records nothing in the field tables, so this
// program is rc 0 with the guard deleted too. The guard's value was measured
// on the twin that DOES populate the table — `let b = &pp.p; *pp.p = 7;` —
// which used to be the second half of this fixture, pinned at rc 0.
//
// ⚠⚠ THAT rc 0 WAS THE DEFECT, NOT THE SPECIFICATION, and the twin has left.
// rustc 1.98.1 --edition 2024 REFUSES the shape with error[E0506] "cannot
// assign to `*pp.p` because it is borrowed" — `&pp.p` is used later through
// `**b`, so the write through the pointer conflicts with it (MEASURED
// 2026-09-21). A pass fixture may not pin a program Rust rejects, so the twin
// is now the soundness-queue program
// tests/soundness/open/derefwrite_rawptr_field_under_live_borrow_admits, which
// the ADR 0028 checker already refuses and which therefore closes at S6. What
// is left here is the FIRST shape, the legal one, and the honest statement that
// it does not by itself prove the guard. The 8 live stdlib sites (cell.logos `*g.value`,
// `*a.value`/`*b.value`, `*self.state`, `*orig.state`; handle.logos
// `*self.id_ctr_p`) are all the first shape, which is why the full build stays
// green either way.
package bc_derefwrite_field_rawptr;
struct P { p: *mut i64 }
fn plain() -> i64 {
    let mut x: i64 = 1i64;
    unsafe {
        let pp: P = P { p: &mut x as *mut i64 };
        *pp.p = 7i64;
        return *pp.p;
    }
}
fn main() -> i32 {
    if plain() != 7i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
